WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.663
Training starts by specifying a loss.

00:00:02.663 --> 00:00:11.989
Follow the lecture's scalar example: a weighted input plus bias goes through sigmoid, then we measure half the squared difference from the target.

00:00:11.989 --> 00:00:14.927
We use squared error here to match the slides.

00:00:14.927 --> 00:00:20.484
Classification networks often use cross entropy instead, and its gradient is different.

00:00:20.484 --> 00:00:24.189
Always derive from the objective actually being optimized.

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:28.660
A formula memorized for another loss can give the wrong training rule.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.036
Take input 2, weight 0.5, bias 0 and target 1.

00:00:33.036 --> 00:00:41.787
The weighted sum is 1, sigmoid gives approximately 0.731059, and the loss is approximately 0.036165.

00:00:41.787 --> 00:00:47.650
Store the preactivation and the prediction during the forward pass.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:58.152
A computational graph represents each input and intermediate quantity as a node, with edges showing direct dependencies.

00:00:58.152 --> 00:01:02.090
The graph makes reuse and branching explicit.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:07.905
We could expand the loss into a long expression and differentiate it symbolically.

00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:11.170
Backpropagation takes a more structured approach.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:17.501
Each node knows its local derivative, and we combine those local derivatives by the chain rule.

00:01:17.501 --> 00:01:25.431
Multiplication depends on both factors, addition passes sensitivity to both inputs, and sigmoid uses its cached output.

00:01:25.431 --> 00:01:31.628
This method applies to large compositions without writing an enormous closed-form derivative.

00:01:31.628 --> 00:01:36.160
The useful product is an efficient numerical derivative computation.

00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:39.276
Start at the loss and move backward.

00:01:39.276 --> 00:01:45.782
Differentiating half the squared error with respect to the prediction gives prediction minus target.

00:01:45.782 --> 00:01:51.508
The sigmoid derivative with respect to its input is prediction times 1 minus prediction.

00:01:51.508 --> 00:01:57.233
Therefore the loss sensitivity at the preactivation is the product of these two factors.

00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:01.918
This is an application of the chain rule, not a new learning assumption.

00:02:01.918 --> 00:02:07.773
At each step, write down which output is being differentiated with respect to which input.

00:02:08.123 --> 00:02:12.061
The preactivation is weight times input plus bias.

00:02:12.061 --> 00:02:16.865
Its derivative with respect to the weight is the input value.

00:02:16.865 --> 00:02:20.409
Its derivative with respect to the bias is 1.

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:27.734
Consequently, the weight gradient has one extra input factor compared with the bias gradient.

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:33.404
For our example the gradients are approximately -0.105754 and -0.052877.

00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:41.043
Their negative signs indicate that small increases in these parameters decrease the loss locally.

00:02:41.393 --> 00:02:44.287
An optimizer now uses these gradients.

00:02:44.287 --> 00:02:50.759
If we update only the weight with learning rate 1, it becomes approximately 0.605754.

00:02:50.759 --> 00:02:55.555
With the bias still 0, the next loss is approximately 0.026320.

00:02:55.555 --> 00:03:02.713
Updating both parameters would produce a different result, so state the convention explicitly.

00:03:02.713 --> 00:03:08.195
Backpropagation computes gradients; gradient descent changes parameters.

00:03:08.195 --> 00:03:16.113
A large learning rate can overshoot, and a negative gradient does not justify an arbitrarily large move.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.711
A graph is not always a single chain.

00:03:19.711 --> 00:03:29.508
If a variable affects the final result through several outgoing paths, its total gradient is the sum of those path contributions.

00:03:29.508 --> 00:03:35.053
The regularized loss on slide 31 has a data-loss path and a penalty path.

00:03:35.053 --> 00:03:43.787
With penalty 0.5 times the squared weight and coefficient lambda, add lambda times the weight to the data gradient.

00:03:43.787 --> 00:03:48.420
At weight 0.5 and lambda 0.1, the extra contribution is 0.05.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:53.471
The multivariate example in the lecture makes the same principle explicit.

00:03:53.471 --> 00:04:00.713
The final function is the second hidden quantity plus the exponential of the product of the two hidden quantities.

00:04:00.713 --> 00:04:06.176
The first hidden quantity is cosine of the input, and the second is the input squared.

00:04:06.176 --> 00:04:10.750
The original input influences the result through both hidden quantities.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:20.850
Differentiate the final function with respect to each hidden quantity separately, then multiply by each hidden quantity's derivative with respect to the input.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:29.460
The first path contributes the exponential of their product, multiplied by the second hidden quantity and minus sine of the input.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:36.766
The second path contributes 1 plus the first hidden quantity times that exponential, multiplied by twice the input.

00:04:36.766 --> 00:04:37.656
Add the paths.

00:04:37.656 --> 00:04:43.437
In code, initialize intermediate gradient accumulators to 0 and add incoming contributions.

00:04:43.437 --> 00:04:49.664
Replacing an earlier gradient contribution instead of adding the new contribution is a common bug.

00:04:49.664 --> 00:04:53.920
The same rule handles a shared weight used many times in a network.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:01.203
For a multilayer network, compute each layer's preactivation and activation in forward order.

00:05:01.203 --> 00:05:13.578
At a hidden layer, collect the sensitivity from all downstream units using the transpose of the next weight matrix, then multiply elementwise by the local activation derivative.

00:05:13.578 --> 00:05:17.423
The lecture writes this as sums over connected neurons.

00:05:17.423 --> 00:05:22.317
The matrix form is the same computation with dimensions made explicit.

00:05:22.317 --> 00:05:27.980
An output sensitivity must be derived from the chosen output activation and loss.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:39.875
The weight gradient of a dense layer is an outer product: the current layer's preactivation sensitivity multiplied by the previous layer's activation transpose.

00:05:39.875 --> 00:05:43.122
The bias gradient is the current sensitivity.

00:05:43.122 --> 00:05:49.111
For a minibatch, sum or average examples consistently with how the loss is defined.

00:05:49.111 --> 00:05:54.594
The graph on slide 32 combines a prediction loss with weight regularization.

00:05:54.594 --> 00:06:02.098
Read it operation by operation: matrix multiplication, activation, output projection, loss, and penalty.

00:06:02.098 --> 00:06:05.850
Branches must join by addition in the backward pass.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:10.637
The general algorithm orders nodes so parents come before children.

00:06:10.637 --> 00:06:13.749
Compute their values in this topological order.

00:06:13.749 --> 00:06:22.356
Seed the final scalar output's sensitivity with 1, then visit nodes in reverse order and propagate contributions to their parents.

00:06:22.356 --> 00:06:28.117
Compute gradients using the parameters from the same forward pass before updating them.

00:06:28.117 --> 00:06:34.142
Updating an early weight too soon can corrupt gradients that still depend on its old value.

00:06:34.142 --> 00:06:39.240
This reverse-mode process is what automatic differentiation systems automate.

00:06:40.034 --> 00:06:41.273
Pause and predict.

00:06:41.273 --> 00:06:46.920
Keep the weight, bias, target and sigmoid objective, but set the input value to 0.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.399
What happens to the weight gradient?

00:06:49.399 --> 00:06:51.947
Must the bias gradient also become 0?

00:06:51.947 --> 00:06:56.905
Then consider a graph where the same variable feeds two multiplications.

00:06:56.905 --> 00:07:03.103
Should its backward contributions be added or averaged merely because there are two paths?

00:07:03.103 --> 00:07:06.064
Draw both paths before checking the answer.

00:07:06.064 --> 00:07:10.954
Graph structure, rather than a slogan, should determine your reasoning.

00:07:11.504 --> 00:07:17.247
The weight gradient is 0 because changing the weight cannot change weight times a 0 input.

00:07:17.247 --> 00:07:22.097
The bias still shifts the preactivation, so its gradient can remain nonzero.

00:07:22.097 --> 00:07:27.585
Contributions from multiple computational paths are added, not automatically averaged.

00:07:27.585 --> 00:07:31.733
Averaging is a separate choice in the definition of a batch loss.

00:07:31.733 --> 00:07:44.624
For practice, check our two analytic gradients using a central finite difference: perturb one parameter slightly up and down, evaluate the loss, and divide the loss difference by twice the perturbation.

00:07:44.974 --> 00:07:47.009
Three rules summarize the part.

00:07:47.009 --> 00:07:50.817
Cache the intermediate values needed by local derivatives.

00:07:50.817 --> 00:07:55.807
Multiply derivatives along each path and add contributions where paths meet.

00:07:55.807 --> 00:07:59.419
Apply parameter updates after the gradient computation.

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:06.378
Your exercise is to add the squared-weight penalty and verify its gradient numerically for several inputs.

00:08:06.378 --> 00:08:18.854
In the final part, we will apply these ideas to a small multilayer network and examine why deeper models can help, how much computation they cost, and why images motivate shared connections.

